1.23.0: wipe, histogram and clipping, loupe, bracket

Four comparison tools on the workspace view, all page-side, so every
render stays byte-identical to 1.22.

Wipe (w): a divider dragged across the view with the current look on
the right and the original, or the look pinned as A, on the left.
Overlays are laid on the displayed image's own rectangle, so they
follow fit and 1:1 modes and resizes.

Histogram and clipping: a per-channel log histogram of what is shown,
sampled at up to 512 px wide from the view's pixels, with the share of
pixels clipped at white per channel and crushed at black; the clipping
button paints them on the view, red for white and blue for black. The
native engine adds /api/stats (the inspect numbers for a file in the
folder), and for raw input the panel reports the sensor's own mean and
clipped share next to the print's, so the two kinds of clipping are
told apart.

Loupe (l): a 320 px window at 1:1 of the full-size render under the
pointer. The full-size render is made once per look (render() takes
overrides and a quiet flag now) and dropped when the look changes.

Bracket: nine renders of the current look stepping one knob -
exposure, develop, strength, age, halation, edge - in a grid over the
view; a click takes that value and re-renders. About a second on the
native engine.

A bug from 1.21.0 fixed on the way: the develop list had been
reordered to start at pull-3, which made pull-3 the page's default
development for every film camera (instant cameras force normal, so
the polaroid sample hid it). The normal entry is now the selected one,
and both page tests check the default.

Tests: test_workspace.mjs (Chromium) drives all four tools - the
histogram text, the clipping overlay, the wipe divider following a
drag and the "vs A" switch, a nine-cell exposure bracket and taking a
cell, the loupe's full-size render and its window; test_serve.sh
covers /api/stats for a raw and a JPEG.

